    status: "recently-explored",
    tagline: "This target has been classified as a steel workboat, resting in 30 feet of water.",
    summary: "Type: Vessel | Class: Shipwreck | Subclass: Metal\nShipwreck City Data\nThis target has been classified as a steel workboat, resting in 30 feet of water. Visibility at the site was very poor, with most of the dive obscured by heavy sediment in the water column. In clearer frames, a flat steel deck section is visible with several vertical support legs or pilings extending down from its underside, along with a small fitting or hardware piece resting on the deck surface.\nPrior Research and Theories\nThis target has been historically explored and logged by Coastal Sensing & Survey under LU21 and . The Lake Union Virtual Museum documents it as a 30-foot steel workboat, noting equipment on board that suggests it may have been a fireboat, with a fish seen swimming inside the cabin in underwater photos credited to diver Dan Warter from 2011. That page links to DCS Films' documentation of the DIT Boat, the same source referenced for target LU022, which may indicate the two targets refer to the same or a related wreck and should be reconciled on a future dive.",
    summarySections: [{heading: "Shipwreck City Data", body: "This target has been classified as a steel workboat, resting in 30 feet of water. Visibility at the site was very poor, with most of the dive obscured by heavy sediment in the water column. In clearer frames, a flat steel deck section is visible with several vertical support legs or pilings extending down from its underside, along with a small fitting or hardware piece resting on the deck surface."}, {heading: "Prior Research and Theories", body: "This target has been historically explored and logged by Coastal Sensing & Survey under LU21 and . The Lake Union Virtual Museum documents it as a 30-foot steel workboat, noting equipment on board that suggests it may have been a fireboat, with a fish seen swimming inside the cabin in underwater photos credited to diver Dan Warter from 2011. That page links to DCS Films' documentation of the DIT Boat, the same source referenced for target LU022, which may indicate the two targets refer to the same or a related wreck and should be reconciled on a future dive."}],
